WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.740
- For more than 25 years,

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:05.250
there's been a medieval castle
under construction in France.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.950
The builders and designers here
are using plans and techniques

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.580
that are as historically accurate
to the 13th century as possible.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.870
Guédelon Castle is experimental archeology
and a tourist attraction.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.550
The techniques rediscovered here,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.150
they're now helping with the
reconstruction of Notre Dame Cathedral.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.730
But I'm here for one very
specific piece of technology

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.570
that I'd never heard of,

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.943
and which to me feels like something

00:00:25.943 --> 00:00:29.790
out of the anachronistic fiction
of Astérix or the Flintstones.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.830
It's called the treadmill crane

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.080
and it is a real thing from history.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.270
Now I'm not an archeologist or a historian

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.690
so I'm going to let someone tell you
about this place, and about this crane,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.180
while I actually go and get some stone

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.770
and learn to power this thing.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.780
- So when we say we're
building a medieval castle,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.130
of course we have to be very clear

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.740
about which period of the
Middle Ages we're interested in.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.660
So we've narrowed down
our area of interest

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.220
to the 13th century.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.680
To be even more precise,
when we laid the first stone

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.840
we said it was the year 1228

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.550
and each year we move forward in history.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.390
So this year on site, you are in
the year of our lord 1253.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.300
So in order to find out what technology
was available at that time,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.130
we have a number of different sources.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.140
We use illuminated manuscripts,
stained glass windows, financial records.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.590
Of course we work with archeologists,
art historians, and castellologists.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:17.991
And so we've got all
that information together

00:01:17.991 --> 00:01:21.090
in order to decide how the
castle should be built.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.410
Sometimes we know how to do it,

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.870
sometimes these skills
have never been lost,

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.940
others have been completely lost

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.550
and we have to then
revive them from scratch.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.550
The castle itself will be finished
in about another 10 years.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.610
So we're taking about 35 years
in all to finish the castle

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.550
which is a really long time
compared to the Middle Ages.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.040
Because one of the reasons
that we're building this castle

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.330
is to show the visiting public.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.940
So half the time my colleagues
are putting down their tools

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.950
in order to explain the work we're doing.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.910
- So here we are at the single drum winch.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.750
- Okay. So that needs to go up there.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.050
- Yes.
- Okay.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:59.250
- We can provide the tower with
those rocks, so we can build it.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.083
- Okay.

00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:02.640
- We might say that we're
building a 13th century castle

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.060
but in terms of health and safety
we're clearly in the 21st century.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.520
So you will see anachronisms on site.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.450
We have hard hats,

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.640
steel toe capped shoes,
protective eyewear,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.400
masks when we're working with
the sandstone from the quarry.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.070
All the scaffolding is heavily reinforced.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.620
So obviously we're not trying to discover

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.290
how many people were killed or
injured in the 13th century.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.720
- So take care of your back.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.250
- The castle that we're
building here today

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.780
is the castle that would've belonged

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.920
to a low ranking local
fairly modest nobleman.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.750
This isn't a royal castle.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.120
It's essentially being
built as a private home

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.030
but there will be defensive
elements within the castle.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:40.410
There'll be a portcullis,

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.070
there'll be murder holes,
arrow loops, crenelated walling.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.290
And all of this even though
there's very little chance

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.967
that the castle would've been attacked
in the middle of the 13th century

00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:52.140
these features are still in place.
You never know.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.373
- There's not any fancy modern pulleys
or anything like that, it's just--

00:02:55.373 --> 00:02:56.280
- No no, it just--

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.920
- Diameter of this
versus diameter of this.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:05.460
- Yes. It's like if you were making
a effort of five flights of stairs.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.060
So if you are walking 10 meters,
it's one meter up.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.130
- It's like a giant gear, basically.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.190
- So we have a couple
of single drummed cranes

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.740
and we also have a double
drummed tread wheel winch.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.900
And the advantage of that
particular piece of machinery

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.900
is that it pivots through 360°.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.810
So that allows us to hoist
loads up from the quarry

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.370
and then spin the machinery around

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.830
and place it exactly where
we need it on the masonry.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.030
With the double drum winch

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.430
we can hoist somewhere in
the region of 500 kilos,

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.140
that's 1,000lb in weight.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.280
With a single drum winch,

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.930
well, there's one person
working so it's half,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.210
250 kilos roughly.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.340
- So I'm going to lift 200 kilos.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.153
- With this one.
- All right.

00:03:43.153 --> 00:03:45.690
- 150, 200.
- Okay.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.260
- Safety is obviously paramount
when we're using this machinery.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.960
So we have someone who's responsible
for applying the brakes.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.130
It means that there's no danger

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.650
of spinning round like
in a washing machine.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.890
We have someone who's at the base,
responsible for charging the cradle.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.920
And then one person who is
in charge of the whole operation

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.640
keeping an eye on how far the
walkers still have to walk.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.410
Because of course when
you're in the machine

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.880
you can't necessarily see.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.760
So it's a very carefully managed process.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.100
This is a technology that
goes back to Roman times

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.550
but which is being used in the
13th century in cathedral construction.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:18.660
We know that they were in use

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.430
because there are a
number of medieval cranes

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.800
that still exist in the
roof timbers of cathedrals.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.040
Canterbury Cathedral, for example,
has a wonderful example of that.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:26.873
- Are you going to be on the brake?

00:04:26.873 --> 00:04:28.200
Does it have a brake?
- Yes.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.236
Look.

00:04:29.236 --> 00:04:32.730
This is normal brake, you can see here.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.678
- Yep, it's just friction on that, that...

00:04:34.678 --> 00:04:38.940
- There's no evidence of a braking system
on these medieval cranes.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:43.530
For safety reasons we've included one,
so that we can stop it if necessary.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.961
Strictly speaking, if all is well,
you just stop walking.

00:04:45.961 --> 00:04:46.794
- Okay.

00:04:46.794 --> 00:04:49.110
- Okay just go ahead and watch your head.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:49.943
- All right.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.410
And right now this won't move
'cause you're on the brake, so...

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.500
- You can look at fixed points,
to help you.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.055
- Yeah 'cause if I'm looking at this...
- Yeah it will be Disneyland!

00:05:02.055 --> 00:05:04.493
Are you ready?
- Ready.

00:05:04.493 --> 00:05:05.326
- Okay.

00:05:05.326 --> 00:05:07.027
- Just walk?
- Walk.

00:05:07.027 --> 00:05:08.479
- Oh! Oh wow.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.130
And look to the side.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:14.610
If I look ahead...
it gets really confusing.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.660
Got to look that way,
got to look that way.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.241
Hey, but it's working!
- Yeah, it's working.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.880
- Oh you're right about that fixed point.
- Yes.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.760
- I looked forward for a bit
and started getting dizzy.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.130
I'm just walking up a
steep hill.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.170
There is some kind of feedback loop,

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.300
the faster I walk the faster it turns,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.860
then the faster it pushes me back.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.232
'Cause of course I'm moving more.

00:05:36.232 --> 00:05:39.723
But then I risk bashing my
head into the axle behind me.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.790
- So these machines are absolutely necessary
in the construction process.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:50.760
We couldn't build the castle
at the rate we are without them.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.120
We hoist somewhere in the region
of three metric tonnes every day.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:54.953
- There we go.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:57.960
- Okay.
- Okay.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:58.793
- And stop!

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.830
Congratulations for your
first medieval crane!

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:08.663
- Thank you.

00:06:08.663 --> 00:06:11.880
I didn't expect quite the
tourist applause there,

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:12.984
thank you very much.

00:06:12.984 --> 00:06:13.817
- Always.

